From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] No starting window; opaque surfaces for software-drawn opaque windows

Both come from a CPU profile of a cold start of Settings on Test V32
(simpleperf, system-wide): 18% of the application's main thread was kernel
page allocation for new window buffers, 6% a libc memset and 10% Skia's
memset32, i.e. every frame was filled twice before anything was drawn.

1. ro.pw3.disable_starting_window: ActivityRecord.showStartingWindow() does
   nothing. A preview window costs its own buffers (6 MB each), a full-screen
   draw and an e-paper update of 134 ms or more, only to be replaced by the
   application's first frame. The previous screen stays until that frame.

2. WindowStateAnimator: with the software canvas for every process (the
   properties ActivityThread uses), FLAG_HARDWARE_ACCELERATED no longer forces
   a TRANSLUCENT surface. An opaque window gets an opaque (RGBX) surface, so
   its canvas is opaque and ViewRootImpl.drawSoftware() does not clear the
   dirty area before each frame.

Not run on the device yet when this patch was written.

--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/am/ActivityRecord.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/am/ActivityRecord.java
@@ -2166,6 +2166,13 @@
             // We don't show starting window for overlay activities.
             return;
         }
+        if (SystemProperties.getBoolean("ro.pw3.disable_starting_window", false)) {
+            // PW3: an e-paper update takes 134 ms or more and every window
+            // buffer is 6 MB of fresh pages. A preview window would be
+            // allocated, drawn and shown only to be replaced by the
+            // application's first frame; the previous screen stays instead.
+            return;
+        }
 
         final CompatibilityInfo compatInfo =
                 service.compatibilityInfoForPackageLocked(info.applicationInfo);
